Kubernetes Readiness vs Liveness Probes

When we deploy an application in Kubernetes, it's not enough that the container is just running.
We also need to ensure it's healthy and ready to serve traffic.
That’s where probes come in.

Today, let’s talk about the two most common ones:
     Readiness Probe
     Liveness Probe
  

Readiness Probe – “Mujhe abhi traffic mat do”
Suppose your app is running but still:
      Loading internal configs
      Making DB connections
      Syncing some background data
      Tab agar user request aa jaye — error milega.

Readiness probe tells Kubernetes – don't send traffic yet!
 Once the app is actually ready, then Kubernetes starts routing requests to it. 


Liveness Probe – “Main zinda hoon ya nahi?”
After a few hours, your app crashes internally or gets stuck (e.g., infinite loop, memory leak).
But from outside, it still "looks running".
Liveness probe checks if the app is alive.
 If not, Kubernetes will automatically restart the pod — no manual effort needed.




Pizza API Example (Freshers ka favourite )
You’ve built a pizza-api backend:
It takes 30 seconds to start because it fetches menu items from DB.
 
Use Readiness Probe so traffic waits until it's really ready.
After a few hours, it freezes due to memory issues.
Use Liveness Probe to auto-restart it.
Bina in probes ke, user ko ya to error milega ya app stuck rahega bina kisi restart ke.
Yahi dikkat interview me bhi poochhi jaati hai 😅



Interview Style Answer:
Q: What’s the difference between Readiness and Liveness Probe?
 A:
 Readiness tells when the app is ready to handle traffic.
 Liveness tells whether the app is still alive or should be restarted.


